    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-87876",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a114450f-bfcd-5db3-9060-fcd067c606fa",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving an Inequality When the Exponential is Compared to Zero or a Negative Number",
      "problem": "Solve the inequality $-4-{e}^{3x}>2.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we isolate the exponential term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4-{e}^{3x} & >2 \\\\ -{e}^{3x} & >6 \\\\ {e}^{3x} & <-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that a positive number raised to any real power is always positive. Since $e>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{3x}>0\n$$\n\nfor every real value of $x.$\n\nTherefore, ${e}^{3x}$ cannot be less than $-6.$ Hence, the inequality\n\n$$\n{e}^{3x}<-6\n$$\n\nhas no solutions.",
