      "math_academy_id": "q-87895",
      "knowledge_point_id": "29435c7e-2913-51cb-b1ca-bb88c5109d0a",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving an Inequality with Exponential Functions on Both Sides",
      "problem": "Solve the inequality $7\\cdot {4}^{1-x}<{3}^{x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\cdot {4}^{1-x} & <{3}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(7\\cdot {4}^{1-x}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡7+(1-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 & <x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡7+\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 & <x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 & <x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\ x & >\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
