    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-115213",
      "knowledge_point_id": "05f94d64-fdf7-55d8-80fb-3988a47ed2d4",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Between Zero and One",
      "problem": "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{4}{(0.62)}^{x+5}\\le 1.$",
      "worked_solution": "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{(0.62)}^{x+5}\\le 1 \\\\ {(0.62)}^{x+5}\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[(0.62)^{x+5}] & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ (x+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.62 & \\le 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.62$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & \\ge \\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.62} \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.62}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "x\\ge \\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.62}+5"
            },
