    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-115218",
      "knowledge_point_id": "05f94d64-fdf7-55d8-80fb-3988a47ed2d4",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Between Zero and One",
      "problem": "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x+1}\\le \\frac{1}{9}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x+1} & \\le \\frac{1}{9} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x+1} & \\le \\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x+1}] & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ (x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ (x+1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ (x+1)(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & \\le -\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & \\ge \\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
